{
  "narrative_body": "A leaked Pentagon email proposing to suspend Spain from NATO positions has made Madrid the named target of formal US punitive planning — and, in doing so, turned it into the rallying point for European solidarity within the alliance.\n\nThe email, prepared by Elbridge Colby, the Pentagon's top policy adviser, outlined options to punish allies that refused basing, access, and overflight rights for the Iran war. Spain was singled out. The goal, the email stated, was \"decreasing the sense of entitlement on the part of the Europeans.\" A NATO official noted promptly that the founding treaty has no suspension mechanism. Pedro Sánchez, attending an informal EU leaders meeting in Nicosia, batted the report away: \"No worries, we are fulfilling our obligations toward NATO.\" But the government had not been caught off guard — three days before Reuters published the email, Mr Sánchez had convened a full National Security Council meeting with an unusually wide ministerial attendance spanning economy, finance, defense, interior, and a dozen other portfolios. No agenda was released; none was needed.\n\nWhat Washington may not have anticipated was the European response. At the Cyprus summit, Dutch Prime Minister Rob Jetten said it was \"crystal clear\" Spain remained a full NATO member. A senior German official said he saw no reason that should change. Most notably, Italy's Giorgia Meloni, who has generally kept close to Washington, called the US-Spain tensions \"not at all positive\" and confirmed Italy had denied its Sigonella airbase for Iran operations — a public break from the American president that Spain's base-denial posture appears to have helped produce. NATO Secretary General Mark Rutte offered implicit criticism of the US pressure. Spain entered this crisis having spent months building a coalition of European allies; the coalition showed up.\n\nEven as it held the line externally, Madrid made two quieter moves. Mr Sánchez held a closed bilateral with Volodymyr Zelensky on the margins of Nicosia — no press, no readout — affirming Ukraine ties at a moment when Spanish credibility within the alliance was under challenge. And on April 23, the ecological transition minister, Sara Aagesen, signed a memorandum of understanding for an electrical interconnector with Ireland. If built, the Atlantic route would ease Spain's dependence on the congested France-Spain electricity link, a bottleneck that has long limited Spain's ability to export its surplus renewable energy into Europe.\n\nAt home, the week brought a different kind of pressure. The Objective, a Madrid outlet, published previously unseen video footage from the October 2016 Socialist Federal Committee meeting — the night Mr Sánchez secured the party leadership. The footage shows ballot urns being moved between rooms while militants shout \"false\" and \"ballot fraud.\" The source of the leak, and its timing, are the analytically significant part: according to The Objective's investigation, only Mr Sánchez and José Luis Ábalos, the former transport minister now facing prosecution, held copies of the footage. Mr Ábalos, with the state prosecutor seeking a 24-year sentence and his Supreme Court testimony imminent, appears to have kept the material as leverage. Emiliano García-Page, a Socialist regional president long hostile to Mr Sánchez, pointed publicly to \"party bosses\" as responsible for the leak. Inside the party, the read was bleak: an attempted internal ballot fraud is almost worse than a corruption case — it destroys the myth of democratic primaries that was Mr Sánchez's founding legitimacy claim.\n\nThe timing could hardly be worse. The May 17 Andalusia regional election is the primary near-term test of whether the government retains any political momentum. That campaign has been defined this week by a \"national priority\" clause in PP-Vox coalition agreements in Extremadura and Aragón — a provision giving local residents priority over immigrants for certain social benefits. Mr Sánchez attacked the pacts at multiple Andalusia campaign stops, calling them gentleman's agreements between Alberto Núñez Feijóo and Santiago Abascal. Mr Feijóo defended the agreements while trying to soften their framing. Mr Abascal had no interest in softening anything: he called Mr Sánchez \"filth\" and the interior minister a \"rat\" at rallies in Cádiz, Lucena, and Málaga, and issued an implicit warning to his own coalition partner — \"don't count the victory against Sánchez as done.\"\n\nThe Aragón coalition, formally constituted this week as Spain's second functioning PP-Vox regional government, illustrated the alliance's internal friction on the very day of its announcement. Alejandro Nolasco, Vox's incoming deputy premier, broke protocol to criticize PP's national headquarters for \"putting sticks in the wheels.\" Génova dismissed it as theater; Vox insiders did not. That pattern — Vox extracting concessions through public pressure rather than private negotiation — is already running in two regional governments. Extended to the national level, it would constrain any PP-led government's room to maneuver well before it took office.\n\nSpain's foreign minister, José Manuel Albares, meanwhile pushed ahead on Gibraltar — meeting Chief Minister Fabián Picardo in Madrid, visiting the La Línea border zone and the fence at La Verja, and inaugurating a public seminar in Algeciras on the EU-UK Gibraltar agreement. The active implementation of that accord, concluded in February, keeps at least one European bilateral running smoothly while Washington plans institutional punishment.",
